## Introduction

A finishing contractor's toolkit tells you everything about the work before it starts. The trade runs on a handful of inexpensive tools — levels, straight edges, floats, trowels, sanding blocks — and the gap between a N15,000 toolkit and a N50,000 one is smaller than the gap between knowing and not knowing how to use them.

This guide lists the tools a serious finishing contractor needs, what each is for, what "good" looks like when buying them, and which items are worth spending extra on versus where budget versions do the same job.

## The Core Kit — What Actually Earns Its Keep

**1. Spirit level (600 mm and 1200 mm).** The two sizes between them check everything from window reveals to wall bays. Good looks like: a readable vial, straight edges when held edge-to-edge against each other, and no bow when sighted along the body. Test any level before paying — levels are the one tool where a cheap bad one actively lies to you. In a market where levels are sometimes stocked hanging in sunlight and warped before sale, the edge-to-edge test (hold the two levels face to face; any light gap on one side means one of them is lying) takes ten seconds and settles the matter.

**2. Straightedge / levelling rule (1.5 to 2 m aluminium).** Not for checking level — for checking *flat*. Run against walls and screeds to find hollows and ridges; also used as a screeding rod. Good looks like: a box-section profile that resists bending, clean machined edges. This single tool defines the difference between walls that look flat and walls that are flat. Sight down its length before buying; a rule with a banana in it passes the banana to every wall it touches.

**3. Plastering / finishing trowel (250 to 300 mm).** The workhorse for skim and plaster. Good looks like: stainless steel (flex with a satisfying spring, no rust), a comfortable handle mount that does not wobble, edges polished rather than stamped. Carbon-steel trowels are cheaper but rust and drag. Buy once, keep clean, never chip concrete with a finishing blade — that is what the pointing trowel is for.

**4. Pointing trowel (100 to 150 mm).** Small jobs, mixing, bucket work, and hacking duties. Budget version is fine — this tool is meant to be abused.

**5. Wide flex knife / joint knife (300 to 400 mm).** The putty tool. Good looks like: real flex in the blade (it should bow under hand pressure, not fight it) and a ground edge. Cheap stiff knives tear the putty film; the wide flex knife is a legitimate place to spend extra.

**6. Wooden and sponge floats.** Wooden float for opening up and keying cement-based coats; sponge (plastic or felt) float for final finishing of screed and render and for tightening skim surfaces. Budget versions fine; replace when the face wears smooth.

**7. Hawk.** The hand-held platform that feeds the trowel. Aluminium or hard plastic, around 300 mm square. Check that the central handle is riveted, not glued. The hawk-and-trowel rhythm is the trade's metronome: material on the hawk, hawk near the wall, trowel loads and applies without the pair ever stopping. A wobbly hawk handle breaks the rhythm, and with it, the evenness of the coat.

**8. Sanding block and pole sander with flat pads.** Hand sanding for corners and detail, pole sanding for walls and ceilings. Grit matters more than gadgetry — keep 80/120 for knocking down ridges, 180/220 for unifying, and 240+ for finishing passes. A flat pad that clamps the sheet at all four corners beats any clever head. Budget sanders flex in the middle; a pad that bows digs two parallel grooves per pass, which is worse than hand sanding.

**9. Measuring tape (5 m or 8 m) and bucket set.** Obvious until the mix ratios start drifting. Calibrated head-pan and buckets keep cement:sand ratios honest — mixing by eye is how coats fail silently. Mark your ratio buckets with paint pen at the fill lines; a bucket with a dented rim is no longer a measure, it is an opinion.

**10. Torch or work lamp.** The raking-light tool. Held nearly parallel to a wall, an ordinary bright torch shows every ripple, pinhole, and lap mark that daylight hides. Nothing in the kit costs less or catches more. Belt it: the torch that lives in the toolbag gets used between stages; the torch that lives in the van does not.

## PPE — Cheap Insurance That Protects the Career

- **N95/P2 dust masks** (boxed, not rationed): plaster and sanding dust do cumulative lung damage; this is the one consumable never to run out of. The damage is silent and permanent — a finishing contractor's lungs meet more fine gypsum and silica in one year than most trades meet in five.
- **Safety goggles**: grit and alkali splashes at eye level are routine in this trade. Cement in the eye is a chemical burn, not an irritation.
- **Gloves**: cement is a strong alkali — repeated skin contact shows up as cement burns and dermatitis. Long sleeves and barrier cream for skim days; the trade's handshake should not cost its skin.
- **Knee pads** and sturdy boots: floor and skirting work punishes joints and toes.

## Worth Paying Extra For vs Where Budget Versions Do Fine

| Spend up here | Budget versions are fine here |
|---|---|
| Spirit level (accuracy is the whole product) | Pointing trowel |
| Straightedge / levelling rule (a bent rule ruins every wall after it) | Buckets, head pan, tape |
| Stainless finishing trowel | Wooden and sponge floats |
| Wide flex knife (blade flexibility is the function) | Hawk |
| Quality sanding system + full grit range | Torch, chalk, string line |
| Dust masks — always boxed | Gloves (replace often instead of buying dear) |

The logic is consistent: pay extra where the tool's *precision or flexibility is the function*; save where it is merely a container, a scraper, or a consumable.

## Kit Configurations by the Job You Chase

**The apprentice kit** covers a first year's work: level, straightedge, budget trowels, flex knife, sanding block, buckets, torch, PPE. The apprentice's edge is not the tools — it is using the straightedge and torch checks on every job while everyone else eyeballs it.

**The contractor's kit** adds redundancy and speed: two levels (one short for reveals), two straightedges (one long for screeds), spare trowel (wet work does not wait for cleanup), pole sander with a dozen pads and three grits, and a dedicated torch that never leaves the bag.

**The site-in-a-box** for multi-room work: the contractor's kit plus mixers or a mixing drill, step ladder, dust sheets, and a vacuum or at minimum a stock of tack cloths and sponges. At this scale the vacuum pays for itself in dust-removal hours within weeks — the stage everyone skips is the stage someone was about to bill anyway.

## Tool-by-Tool Technique Notes

The tools teach the technique if you know what to listen for:

- **The trowel angle** — steep to load, progressively shallower to flatten, near-flat to polish. When the wall is truly flat, the blade stops moving material and starts making a hiss. That sound is the job finishing itself.
- **The hawk stays moving** — parked hawks let the skim set in the pile; thirty seconds of idle is a skin of set material entering the next trowel load as a hard streak.
- **The float's direction** — wooden float in tight circles to key; sponge float in overlapping sweeps for the final tightening. Circles for grip, sweeps for density.
- **The flex knife loads from the tub, not the tub-lid** — scraping putty off a dried lid contaminates the pass with hard grit, which then drags a white scratch through the entire stroke.
- **The pole sander stays flat** — tilting it turns the edge into a gouge. If the pad corners wear faster than the middle, the operative is tilting.
- **The tape measure pulls diagonals too** — square corners are checked at diagonals, not just sides, and out-of-square rooms betray themselves in the last row of tiles and the last sheet of anything.

## Care — The Free Part of a Long-Service Toolkit

- Wash trowels, floats, and buckets immediately after use. Anything with set plaster on it is half-ruined and contaminates every later mix with hard streaks.
- Never use the finishing trowel as a chisel, tin opener, or concrete breaker — a nicked edge prints its signature on every wall thereafter.
- Store levels and rules flat, hanging, or on edge under no load. A bowed straightedge quietly fails every wall it touches.
- Wipe grit off threads and adjusting mechanisms; grit destroys them faster than use does.
- Keep grits separated and labelled. Grabbing 120 thinking it is 240 is how polished walls get scratched.

## Tool Quality vs Skill — Where Results Actually Come From

The kit above fits in two bags and costs less than one re-skim of a living room. The tools do not make the finish — the checks do: the straightedge pass that maps the wall before coating, the trowel-angle discipline that feathers ridges away, the raking-light pass between every stage. A skilled contractor with budget tools will out-finish an unskilled one with imported equipment every time. Buy the honest kit, then spend the real money on the habit of inspection.

The market test proves it: watch two contractors quoting the same room. The one who arrives with a straightedge and a torch and spends ten silent minutes at the walls is quoting a wall. The one who arrives with a catalogue of imported tools and quotes from the doorway is quoting a hope.

## A Day With the Kit — the Tools in Sequence

The kit earns in a specific order, matched to the finishing sequence (the [smooth wall guide](/learn/how-to-achieve-smooth-wall-finish) explains the stages in full):

**Arrival and mapping (tape, straightedge, level, chalk, torch).** Ten minutes: deviations recorded per wall, hollows chalked, tap-test complete. Everything downstream — material quantities, labour days, where the re-plastering goes — is decided by this mapping. The tools involved cost almost nothing and produce the most valuable information on the site.

**Repair and skim (pointing trowel, hawk, finishing trowel, buckets, floats).** The trade proper: hawk feeds trowel, trowel loads wall, angle drops as the coat firms, boundaries feathered. The wooden float only appears if the build-up is cement-based and needs keying; the sponge float for the final tightening pass.

**Sanding and inspection (sanding block, pole sander, grit ladder, torch, sponge).** Knock down with 100/120, unify with 180/220, torch check between grits, dust with the damp sponge. The pole sander owns the field areas; the hand block owns corners, reveals, and behind radiators.

**Putty and prime (flex knife, putty tub, brush and roller, primer).** One scraped pass, dry, fine sand, dust, prime. The flex knife does in thirty seconds what a stiff blade cannot do at all — lay putty without tearing it back off the wall.

**Final coats (brush, roller, torch).** Cut in, roll the W, keep the wet edge, torch-check the first coat before the last. Then the cleaning ritual, which is the first act of the next job, not the last act of this one.

A contractor who cannot narrate their day in this tool order is improvising; a contractor who can, quotes accurately, because every line in the quote maps to a stage and a tool and a number of hours.

## Buying at the Market — the Inspection Checklist

Building materials markets reward inspection. Before paying:

- **Levels:** edge-to-edge test against each other; sight along the body for bow; vial readable and unclouded.
- **Straightedges:** sight down the length from both ends; check both machined edges with a fingernail for dents; flex it gently — it should resist, not bend and stay bent.
- **Trowels:** spring the blade by hand — it should return true; run a fingertip along the edges — polished, not stamped sharp; shake the handle mount — no rattle.
- **Flex knives:** bow the blade against the counter — a good one takes a curve and springs back; check the ground edge for uniformity along its full width.
- **Floats:** flat face, evenly attached handle; sponge floats should be dense enough to spring back instantly.
- **Sanding pads:** the backing must be flat and the clamps functional — press the corners; flimsy corners mean a bowed pad after one wall.
- **Buckets and measures:** square rims, no dents. A measure with a warped rim is an approximation, and ratios built on approximations fail at scale.

If a seller will not let you test the tool before buying, that is the inspection result.

## The Consumables Budget — the Kit's Running Costs

The durable kit is bought once; the real monthly spend is consumables, and planning them is part of owning the kit:

- **Sandpaper** in the three grit bands, bought by the sleeve — buying sheets one at a time triples their price and guarantees running out mid-wall.
- **Dust masks**, boxed, replaced when breathing resistance rises — a mask worn past its life is a decoration.
- **Mixing water containers** — clean water is a finishing material; the container for it deserves its own bucket, never the cement-stained one.
- **Sponges and tack cloths** for the dust stage — the cheapest stage to do properly and the most expensive to skip.
- **Masking tape and dust sheets** — the protection that makes the difference between a professional edge and a "we will touch that up later" corner.

A contractor who budgets consumables keeps the sequence; a contractor who runs out of grit at 180 sands the rest of the room with worn paper and calls the result finished. The wall keeps the receipt.

