## Quick Answers to the Questions Buyers Ask

**"How much should a starter finishing kit cost?"** With honest budget choices on the table above — the straight tools bought well, everything else adequate — a working kit lands between a modest and a moderate spend, and the difference between it and the imported version buys the dust masks and sandpaper you will actually consume. Buy the kit, not the label.

**"Corded drill-mixer or hand-mix?"** Hand-mixing works for small batches, but for skim jobs a mixing drill and paddle pays for itself in consistency — lumps in skim are sanding hours. If the budget allows one power tool, this is the one; the second is a good work light.

**"Are telescopic levels and laser levels worth it?"** For finishing itself, no — the straightedge is the instrument of this trade, because finishing measures *flatness*, not level. Lasers earn their money in screeding and layouts; the wall finisher's laser is the torch.

**"How long should a good trowel last?"** Indefinitely with clean habits — stainless trowels outlive their owners. The retirement event is usually a dropped edge on concrete, not wear. If you are buying your third trowel in a year, the problem is not the trowels.

**"What is the most commonly missing tool on Nigerian sites?"** The torch, then the straightedge — the two inspection tools. Their absence is why so many freshly painted walls ripple at 8 p.m., and why the same rooms get repainted every eighteen months. A finishing trade without inspection tools is a finishing trade selling paint twice.

## Closing

The finishing trade's toolkit is short and honest: straight tools to check truth, flexible blades to apply and fill, grit to refine, light to verify, and dust protection to keep earning. Spend up on the straight and flexible items, run budget on the consumables, and clean everything the day it is used. To size the materials those tools will apply on your next job, use the [finishing estimator](/finish-estimator); for the sequence of coats they apply, see the [complete guide to interior wall finishing](/learn/complete-guide-interior-wall-finishing).
